From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: an elevated program leaves the user's HKCU the user's (5948)

server: an elevated program leaves the user's HKCU the user's

Python 3.13 and the .NET Desktop Runtime would not install for an account
that had run an elevated installer since the machine started: their Burn
bundles stopped at once with code 5 ("Failed to open uninstall registry key",
0x80070005). The account could not create any key under its own
HKCU\Software, nor open HKCU\Software itself (qa4, bob).

An elevated program runs as the SYSTEM account with the user's HKCU, as on
Windows. 0024 lets SYSTEM stamp its default descriptor onto an existing key
that has none (meant for system.reg's keys), and gives each key a process
creates the descriptor of its token. In the user's hive that made
HKCU\Software, and every key the elevated program created, "Users read,
Administrators full": the user lost their own settings until the server
restarted (descriptors are not saved), and an elevated install at the next
boot did it again.

SYSTEM now neither stamps nor gives its descriptor to a key in a user's hive
(\Registry\User\...); such a key stays without one, like every key of the
hive loaded from its file. A user's own new keys still get the user's token
descriptor. Gate test-userhive; mutant SG_MUTANT_USERHIVE_STAMP.
---
--- a/server/registry.c
+++ b/server/registry.c
@@ -660,6 +660,25 @@
     return ret;
 }
 
+/* Stained Glass OS: whether a key is in a user's hive (\Registry\User\...). Elevated programs
+ * run as the SYSTEM account with the user's own HKCU, as on Windows; what they create or touch
+ * there must stay the user's. */
+static int sg_in_user_hive( struct object *obj )
+{
+    static const WCHAR userW[] = {'\\','R','e','g','i','s','t','r','y','\\','U','s','e','r','\\'};
+    data_size_t len = 0;
+    WCHAR *name;
+    int ret;
+
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_USERHIVE_STAMP
+    return 0;
+#endif
+    if (!(name = obj->ops->get_full_name( obj, &len ))) { clear_error(); return 0; }
+    ret = len > sizeof(userW) && !memicmp_strW( name, userW, sizeof(userW) );
+    free( name );
+    return ret;
+}
+
 static int sg_is_display_config_key( struct object *obj )
 {
     data_size_t len = 0;
@@ -1133,11 +1152,14 @@
          * Policies would otherwise stay unprotected even after sg-prefix-init
          * "creates" it. Only the SYSTEM account (the prefix owner, or root) may
          * do this, and only to a key that has no descriptor yet, so it cannot
-         * be used to seize a key from another user. */
+         * be used to seize a key from another user. Never in a user's hive: an
+         * elevated program runs as SYSTEM with the user's HKCU, and the machine
+         * descriptor stamped on HKCU\Software ("users read") locked the user out of
+         * their own settings until the server restarted (qa4 2026-10-21). */
         if (sg_system_prefix && !sd && current && !key->obj.sd &&
             (current->process->peer_uid == 0 ||
              current->process->peer_uid == sg_system_prefix_uid) &&
-            current->process->token)
+            current->process->token && !sg_in_user_hive( &key->obj ))
         {
             static const struct security_descriptor empty_sd;
             set_sd_defaults_from_token( &key->obj, &empty_sd,
@@ -1172,7 +1194,14 @@
          * Only when there is a current thread: the registry is also built
          * during server startup, while system.reg is loaded, and there is no
          * token to derive anything from then. */
-        if (sg_system_prefix && !sd && current && current->process->token && !key->obj.sd)
+        /* ... but not one SYSTEM (an elevated program) creates in a user's hive: SYSTEM's
+         * descriptor (users read) locked the user out of the key, and of all its subkeys
+         * (an elevated installer's HKCU\Software\...\Uninstall: every Burn bundle the user
+         * ran afterwards failed to start, 0x80070005; qa4 2026-10-21). It stays without one,
+         * as every key of the hive loaded from its file. */
+        if (sg_system_prefix && !sd && current && current->process->token && !key->obj.sd &&
+            !((current->process->peer_uid == 0 || current->process->peer_uid == sg_system_prefix_uid) &&
+              sg_in_user_hive( &key->obj )))
         {
             static const struct security_descriptor empty_sd;
             set_sd_defaults_from_token( &key->obj, &empty_sd,
